{"custom_id":"v__ap_chemistry_fx27_k0","ok":true,"model":"claude-opus-5-5","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg5H9u3ncYSesLJe2xMPK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Option **[3]** is the only error that makes the calculated magnitude too high. For an exothermic dissolution, the calculated values are q = m·c·ΔT and |ΔH| = q / n(salt).\n\n- **[0] Wrong (gives a lower value):** The heat measured comes from the 5.00 g that actually dissolved. Dividing it by the moles in 5.50 g uses too many moles, so |ΔH| comes out too low.\n- **[1] Wrong (gives a lower value):** Heat lost through the cup walls makes ΔT smaller, so q and |ΔH| come out too low.\n- **[2] Wrong (gives a lower value):** If the initial temperature reads 1.0 °C too high, the calculated ΔT (final minus initial) is 1.0 °C too small. That makes q and |ΔH| too low.\n- **[3] Correct (gives a higher value):** The measured ΔT is real and comes from 100 g of water. Using 110 g in q = m·c·ΔT overstates q by 10%, so |ΔH| comes out too high."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012YVcJhjsN5XHF51arGtJDx","name":"submit_solution","input":{"questions":[{"q":1,"chosen_index":3,"correct_options":[3],"unique":true,"missing_information":"none","work":"|dH| = mcΔT/n. [0] Recorded moles exceed the moles actually dissolved, so |dH| is too low. [1] Heat loss makes ΔT smaller, so |dH| is too low. [2] An initial temperature read too high makes ΔT smaller, so |dH| is too low. [3] The real ΔT comes from 100 g of water, but using 110 g overstates q, so |dH| is too high."}]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":955,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":837,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":247},"service_tier":"batch","i