{"custom_id":"v__ap_biology_fx08_k0","ok":true,"model":"claude-opus-5-5","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg5HATMV3PvAeUMMTFhwc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HEPv6MDv7CrwrM2nuwF8iT","name":"submit_review","input":{"answerable_from_shown_text":true,"missing_information":"None. The stem says the UV doses are low enough that most cells survive, which rules out the competing prediction that colonies drop at long exposures because cells die. That leaves one well-supported prediction for (C).","parts":[{"label":"A","your_answer":"A change in the DNA nucleotide sequence (a substitution, insertion or deletion) changes the mRNA codons transcribed from it. This can change the amino acid sequence of the protein: one amino acid may be swapped, a frameshift may change many amino acids, or a premature stop codon may cut the protein short. A different primary structure can change how the protein folds, and so its shape and function.","agrees_with_reference":true,"issue":""},{"label":"B","your_answer":"Independent variable: how long the cells are exposed to UV light (0, 10, 20 or 30 s). Dependent variable: the number of colonies (antibiotic-resistant cells) that grow on the antibiotic plates.","agrees_with_reference":true,"issue":""},{"label":"C","your_answer":"The longer the UV exposure, the more colonies will grow on the antibiotic plates. The 0 s plate should have the fewest colonies, possibly none.","agrees_with_reference":true,"issue":""},{"label":"D","your_answer":"UV light damages DNA (for example, by forming thymine dimers), which leads to mutations when the DNA is repaired or copied. Longer exposure causes more DNA damage and more mutations, so more cells are likely to get a mutation that makes them resistant to the antibiotic. Because most cells survive, these resistant mutants can grow into colonies.","agrees_with_reference":true,"issue":""}]},"caller":{"t