{"custom_id":"v__ap_biology_fx06_k0","ok":true,"model":"claude-opus-5-5","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg5HA6GUZZpy6ZExWqR8g","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Rpd9vU1xQMWFCaUVVm9qzP","name":"submit_review","input":{"answerable_from_shown_text":true,"missing_information":"","parts":[{"label":"A","your_answer":"Water potential (Ψ) is the potential energy of water per unit volume relative to pure water, set by solute potential plus pressure potential (Ψ = Ψs + Ψp). It measures how strongly water tends to move. Water moves from higher (less negative) to lower (more negative) water potential.","agrees_with_reference":true,"issue":""},{"label":"B","your_answer":"Put sucrose molarity (M) on the x-axis (the independent variable) and percent change in mass (%) on the y-axis (the dependent variable). Use evenly spaced scales and make the y-axis run from about -25 to +10 so the negative values fit. Plot all six points and connect them or draw a best-fit line. The points fall on a straight line with a negative slope.","agrees_with_reference":true,"issue":""},{"label":"C","your_answer":"(i) The data fit a straight line, % change = 7 - 28 × M, which crosses 0% at about 0.25 M (between 0.2 and 0.4 M). (ii) Null hypothesis: sucrose molarity has no effect on the percent change in mass of the potato cores.","agrees_with_reference":true,"issue":""},{"label":"D","your_answer":"The cores would lose more mass in 0.4 M NaCl than the -4.2% in 0.4 M sucrose. NaCl splits into two ions (i = 2), so the solution has about twice as many solute particles. Since Ψs = -iCRT, the solute potential, and so the water potential, of the NaCl solution is lower (more negative). More water therefore moves out of the cores by osmosis.","agrees_with_reference":true,"issue":""}]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage